# Each word tells the reader a step is easy or a behavior is small,
# which the reader judges for themselves. Neither needs a guard for a
# product name or a mention of the word, since both are rare in
# technical prose, and the skill wants both gone from a sentence about
# behavior.
#
# `simply` skips a following ` put`, since the restatement rule
# reports `Simply put`. The guard sits glued to the word so it reads
# the token right after it. `Put simply` and `To put it simply` count
# here instead, because no pattern can look back from `simply` to the
# `put` before it.
#
# After a negation the word carries a contrast (`it is not simply an
# offset`), and deleting it flips the claim, so the not-only rule's
# shape applies instead. Weir has no lookbehind, so the first branch
# consumes the token before the word, which must not be a negation,
# and the filter narrows the finding back to the word. A space in a
# pattern is itself a whitespace token, so `!@neg @word` reads a word,
# a space, and the minimizer. That branch needs a token before the
# word, so a sentence that opens with one counts through the other
# two, which need a verb or a glued comma after it (`Simply run make`,
# `Simply, the cache is a map`). Those two also fire after a negation
# when a verb follows (`is not simply copied`), a shape rare enough to
# leave.
expr neg [not, never, isn't, aren't, wasn't, weren't]
expr word [(simply!(( )put)), merely]
expr main [<(!@neg @word), @word>, (simply[(( )<VERB, !put>), (,)]), (merely[(( )VERB), (,)])]
let message "Minimizer. Delete the word and let the reader judge the weight."
let description "Flags simply and merely, which tell the reader a step is easy or a behavior is small."
let becomes ""
let kind "Style"
let scope "Sentence"
allows "Flush writes the buffer to disk."
allows "Simply put, the cache is a map."
allows "The offset is not simply an index into the token list."
allows "It isn't merely a cache."
